  "_note": "RUN 3 of the series -- diurnal / crowd-density probe. 120 repeats of the series ANCHOR condition (incremental, adv 1s, 8s dwell, 20 windows, +9 dBm) back to back over ~5.3h, one time-point every ~160s. Nothing varies but time, so deliverability variation is attributable to ambient finder density alone: join each cell's deliverability to the mean finders in density.csv over its send window. This answers the question every other run begs -- whether the ~99.9% ceiling belongs to the channel or to this site at this hour -- and because its cell IS the anchor, it calibrates the anchor readings sprinkled through runs 1, 2 and 4 into a density curve rather than isolated spot checks. Best run across a busy->quiet transition. Ground truth: resweep.py. ANCHOR CELLS: cells named a###_ref are the series' shared reference condition (incremental, adv 1s, 8s dwell, 20 windows, +9 dBm) -- the same cell the density matrix repeats 120 times. One opens every run and one follows every few blocks, ~10% of the run. They are not part of this matrix's design; they exist so any two nights can be compared against a condition they actually share, and so a night whose ambient density was unusual announces itself instead of silently biasing the comparison. Analyse them across runs first, then read the run's own contrast. ANTENNA STATE: directional antenna FITTED -- the series BASELINE state. Runs 1, 3 and 4 all use it, so their anchor cells form one comparable chain; only the txpower_dwell_noant half departs from it. If you run this with the antenna off, its anchors do NOT compare against the rest of the series -- record it and say so.",
